{"id":78341548381166,"type":"artist","name":"Uncle Tupelo","artist":null,"year":1987,"weight":14,"info":{"tags":["Alt-country","americana","country","indie","alt country"],"country":"US","begin":"1987","end":"1994-05","kind":"Group","similar":["Son Volt","Whiskeytown","Wilco","The Jayhawks","Golden Smog","Old 97's","Billy Bragg & Wilco","Drive-By Truckers","Blue Mountain","Jay Farrar"],"img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Uncle_Tupelo_star.jpg?width=500","bio":"Uncle Tupelo fue una banda de country alternativo. Existió hasta 1994. Tras su separación Jeff Twedy pasaría a formar Wilco mientras Jay Farrar formaría Son Volt. \n\nUncle Tupelo destacaron en su papel de renovadores del country, siendo una de las bandas que dieron paso a la actual pléyade de grupos instalados en las coordenadas del alt-country."},"works":[{"id":152301199432664,"type":"album","name":"No Depression","artist":"Uncle Tupelo","year":1990,"weight":16,"yt":"bu6nzpkoFyo","src":"books,charts,musthear","from":{"books":[{"name":"Scaruffi","title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000 (Piero Scaruffi)"}],"podcasts":[],"lists":[{"id":90391435651867,"name":"Sputnikmusic Best Albums 1990","rank":117,"scaruffi":null},{"id":109535037444116,"name":"RYM Top — Rock — Country Rock","rank":81,"scaruffi":null},{"id":118798969175346,"name":"RYM Top — Country — Alt-Country","rank":133,"scaruffi":null}],"n_lists":3}},{"id":211105325410550,"type":"album","name":"Still Feel Gone","artist":"Uncle Tupelo","year":1991,"weight":12,"yt":"ZkaQpGoKx6M","src":"charts,musthear","from":{"books":[],"podcasts":[],"lists":[{"id":222945056519976,"name":"Sputnikmusic Best Albums 1991","rank":101,"scaruffi":null},{"id":103254340346721,"name":"RYM Top — Regional Music — Americana","rank":83,"scaruffi":null},{"id":109535037444116,"name":"RYM Top — Rock — Country Rock","rank":55,"scaruffi":null},{"id":118798969175346,"name":"RYM Top — Country — Alt-Country","rank":91,"scaruffi":null}],"n_lists":4}},{"id":213843280513578,"type":"album","name":"March 16 to 20, 1992","artist":"Uncle Tupelo","year":1992,"weight":6,"yt":"ppiol9oetOk","src":"charts,musthear","from":{"books":[],"podcasts":[],"lists":[{"id":55316938071026,"name":"Sputnikmusic Best Albums 1992","rank":58,"scaruffi":null}],"n_lists":1}},{"id":179940374768389,"type":"album","name":"March 16-20 1992","artist":"Uncle Tupelo","year":1992,"weight":15,"yt":"ppiol9oetOk","src":"books,charts,musthear","from":{"books":[{"name":"Scaruffi","title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000 (Piero Scaruffi)"}],"podcasts":[],"lists":[{"id":13018320481353,"name":"Scaruffi 1990s","rank":1296,"scaruffi":7.0},{"id":103254340346721,"name":"RYM Top — Regional Music — Americana","rank":42,"scaruffi":7.0},{"id":118798969175346,"name":"RYM Top — Country — Alt-Country","rank":51,"scaruffi":7.0},{"id":181312163526721,"name":"RYM Top — Country","rank":135,"scaruffi":7.0}],"n_lists":4}},{"id":199029845296049,"type":"album","name":"Anodyne","artist":"Uncle Tupelo","year":1993,"weight":18,"yt":"KZ4j5EV2hU0","src":"books,charts,musthear","from":{"books":[{"name":"Scaruffi","title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000 (Piero Scaruffi)"}],"podcasts":[],"lists":[{"id":172950565318915,"name":"Holy Trinities @LadoB","rank":1,"scaruffi":null},{"id":59251635065665,"name":"Sputnikmusic Best Albums 1993","rank":75,"scaruffi":null},{"id":103254340346721,"name":"RYM Top — Regional Music — Americana","rank":49,"scaruffi":null},{"id":118798969175346,"name":"RYM Top — Country — Alt-Country","rank":56,"scaruffi":null}],"n_lists":5}}],"members":[],"member_of":[],"genres":[],"album_genres":[{"id":267868162516031,"type":"genre","name":"Alt-Country","n":4},{"id":210192360016800,"type":"genre","name":"Alternative Rock","n":4},{"id":185670346256268,"type":"genre","name":"rock","n":4},{"id":166173220937290,"type":"genre","name":"Americana","n":3},{"id":90510611927654,"type":"genre","name":"Country Rock","n":2}],"labels":[],"album_labels":[],"own_scenes":[],"scenes":[{"id":267868162516031,"name":"Alt-country","book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","start":1988,"end":2000,"desc":"La revolución iniciada en Chicago por Souled American y Uncle Tupelo (No Depression: baladas country con la furia del hardcore) se convierte en los noventa en un género propio: sus herederos abandonan el hardcore por los sonidos más espartanos y tradicionales. Son Volt de Jay Farrar y Wilco de Jeff Tweedy (Being There, Yankee Hotel Foxtrot); Will Oldham (Palace) arranca el movimiento; Freakwater, Ida, Handsome Family, Retsin, Blue Mountain, Whiskeytown, Grant Lee Buffalo, Citizens' Utilities, Willard Grant Conspiracy. El «chamber folk» de Lambchop, Lullaby For The Working Class, Geraldine Fibbers y Pinetop Seven; el country-rock de Volebeats, Ass Ponys, Sixteen Horsepower, Old 97's y los terroristas Grifters; el folk-rock de Swell, Counting Crows, Cracker, Cake, Hootie & The Blowfish; los Black Crowes; Belle And Sebastian, Arab Strap y los polacos Atman.","color":"#f59e0b","family":"Raíces: rock'n'roll, country y roots","link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#alt-country"}],"book_tracks":[{"id":152301199432664,"artist":"Uncle Tupelo","artist_id":78341548381166,"title":"No Depression","year":1990,"ess":0,"yt":"EGuLJ_qtCq4","kind":"a","book":{"id":8,"title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/"},"scene":"Alt-country","scene_id":267868162516031,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#alt-country","chapters":["Sección 5.17"],"pages":[[1,194,null]]},{"id":179940374768389,"artist":"Uncle Tupelo","artist_id":78341548381166,"title":"March 16-20 1992","year":1992,"ess":0,"yt":"pb62aYXM7U0","kind":"a","book":{"id":8,"title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/"},"scene":"Alt-country","scene_id":267868162516031,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#alt-country","chapters":["Sección 5.17"],"pages":[[1,194,null]]},{"id":199029845296049,"artist":"Uncle Tupelo","artist_id":78341548381166,"title":"Anodyne","year":1993,"ess":0,"yt":"KZ4j5EV2hU0","kind":"a","book":{"id":8,"title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/"},"scene":"Alt-country","scene_id":267868162516031,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#alt-country","chapters":["Sección 5.17"],"pages":[[1,194,null]]}],"mentions":[{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":194,"text":"…texture-oriented instrumental jamming, reinvented their sound around Tuma's guitar. Led by singer-songwriters Jay Farrar and Jeff Tweedy, Uncle Tupelo (1) pretty much invented a new genre when they released No Depression (1990), a collection of country ballads played with the fury of hardcore.…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":246,"text":"…by the no wave and Royal Trux's Twin Infinitives. Maryland's Velocity Girl (2) synthesized the new sounds of their time: Sonic Youth's noise-rock, Uncle Tupelo's alt-country, Pavement's lo-fi dynamics. The dissonant pop of Copacetic (1993) was a study in contrast: effervescent tempos, wildly off-key…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":277,"text":"…6.14 Roots-rock in the age of Alt-country Alt Country The revolution in roots-rock that began in the late 1980s in Chicago with Souled American and Uncle Tupelo continued in the 1990s and began a new genre altogether. Those bands had rediscovered country and folk music for the hardcore generation: their descendants dumped hardcore for the most spartan and traditional of sounds. Uncle Tupelo bred two offshoots. Jay Farrar's Son Volt were mostly a vehicle for their leader's philosophizing: Trace (1995) was a concept album that analyzed the collective subconscious of the people of the Mississippi river. Jeff Tweedy's Wilco (2) expanded Uncle Tupelo's vocabulary towards the Byrds' folk-rock, Neil Young's mournful ballads, the Rolling Stones' drunk rhythm'n'blues, the Band's domestic gospel-rock,…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":278,"text":"…of primitive folk music but evolved with Old Paint (1995) to deliver a bleak vision of humankind in a stark, neutral style. Alt-country, or (from Uncle Tupelo's classic album) \"no-depression folk\", ruled the second half of the decade, and influenced even bands that had little to do with the acoustic…"}],"podcast":[],"awards":[],"concerts":[],"xref":[],"charts":[{"id":152301199432664,"type":"album","name":"No Depression","list_id":118798969175346,"list":"RYM Top — Country — Alt-Country","src":"musthear","rank":133,"year":1990,"note":null,"yt":"bu6nzpkoFyo","scaruffi":null},{"id":152301199432664,"type":"album","name":"No Depression","list_id":109535037444116,"list":"RYM Top — Rock — Country Rock","src":"musthear","rank":81,"year":1990,"note":null,"yt":"bu6nzpkoFyo","scaruffi":null},{"id":152301199432664,"type":"album","name":"No Depression","work_year":1990,"list_id":90391435651867,"list":"Sputnikmusic Best Albums 1990","src":"charts","rank":117,"year":1990,"note":null,"yt":"bu6nzpkoFyo","scaruffi":null},{"id":211105325410550,"type":"album","name":"Still Feel Gone","list_id":118798969175346,"list":"RYM Top — Country — Alt-Country","src":"musthear","rank":91,"year":1991,"note":null,"yt":"ZkaQpGoKx6M","scaruffi":null},{"id":211105325410550,"type":"album","name":"Still Feel Gone","list_id":103254340346721,"list":"RYM Top — Regional Music — Americana","src":"musthear","rank":83,"year":1991,"note":null,"yt":"ZkaQpGoKx6M","scaruffi":null},{"id":211105325410550,"type":"album","name":"Still Feel Gone","list_id":109535037444116,"list":"RYM Top — Rock — Country Rock","src":"musthear","rank":55,"year":1991,"note":null,"yt":"ZkaQpGoKx6M","scaruffi":null},{"id":211105325410550,"type":"album","name":"Still Feel Gone","work_year":199